1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,451
This is STAT 331, video 21. It covers Lecture 11: the ANOVA decomposition, R squared, and F tests.

2
00:00:09,901 --> 00:00:29,101
After this video, you can split the total variation of y into two parts. You can compute R squared and fill an ANOVA table. You can test the whole model, a group of covariates, and a general linear hypothesis. And you can solve the three practice questions of Lecture 11.

3
00:00:29,551 --> 00:00:46,788
First, a short recap from slides 7 and 8. The model is y equals X beta plus epsilon. The errors are multivariate normal with mean 0 and variance sigma squared times I. X has a column of ones and p covariates.

4
00:00:47,238 --> 00:01:06,993
We estimate beta by least squares: beta hat is X transpose X inverse, times X transpose y. sigma hat squared is e transpose e over n minus p plus 1. The standard error of beta j hat uses V j j, a diagonal entry of V.

5
00:01:07,443 --> 00:01:19,389
We have two tests. The t test is for one coefficient. The F test is for a joint null hypothesis, beta star equals 0, where beta star has length q.

6
00:01:19,839 --> 00:01:34,773
In Lecture 10, indicators, interactions and polynomial terms only changed the columns of X. So all these tools still apply. This lecture adds a new way to look at the F test: the sums of squares.

7
00:01:35,223 --> 00:02:03,788
Slide 15 recalls the fit. beta hat gives the fitted values y hat, which equal H times y. H is the hat matrix. The residuals e are y minus y hat, which equal I minus H, times y. The plot uses the mercury data and model M1, with fishpart and weight. Each blue point is one fisherman: the fitted value across, the observed MeHg up. The green line is y equals y hat.

8
00:02:04,238 --> 00:02:18,147
The variability in the outcome is how far the y i values are from their mean, y bar. The purple dashed line is y bar, 3.644. The points spread far above and below it.

9
00:02:18,597 --> 00:02:26,000
ANOVA means analysis of variance. It asks: how much of this variability does our model explain?

10
00:02:26,450 --> 00:02:37,373
Slide 16 is drawn in class. Here we draw it with the mercury data. Each point has an observed value y i and a fitted value y hat i.

11
00:02:37,823 --> 00:02:40,404
The purple line is the mean, y bar.

12
00:02:40,854 --> 00:02:49,430
Take one subject. Its total deviation is y i minus y bar: the black bar, from the purple line up to the point.

13
00:02:49,880 --> 00:03:02,253
The green bar goes from y bar up to the fitted value y hat i, on the green line. This part, y hat i minus y bar, is the part that the model explains.

14
00:03:02,703 --> 00:03:11,727
The orange bar goes from y hat i up to y i. This is the residual e i: the part that the model does not explain.

15
00:03:12,177 --> 00:03:23,313
So the total deviation is the residual plus the explained part. This holds for every subject. Next, we square these parts and add them over all subjects.

16
00:03:23,763 --> 00:03:34,153
Slide 17 defines the total sum of squares, S S Total. It is the sum over all subjects of y i minus y bar, squared.

17
00:03:34,603 --> 00:03:43,051
Write each deviation as the two parts from the picture: y i minus y hat i, plus y hat i minus y bar.

18
00:03:43,501 --> 00:03:51,501
Expand the square, as a plus b, squared, equals a squared plus b squared plus 2 a b. This gives three sums.

19
00:03:51,951 --> 00:04:09,423
The first sum is the residual sum of squares, S S Res. The second is the regression sum of squares, S S Reg. The third sum, the cross term, is 0. The next slide shows why. So S S Total equals S S Res plus S S Reg.

20
00:04:09,873 --> 00:04:27,921
y i minus y hat i is the residual e i. So the cross term is the sum of e i times y hat i minus y bar. The plane in the picture is the column space of X. It holds every vector X times b, and the ones vector.

21
00:04:28,371 --> 00:04:47,208
In vector form, the sum is e transpose, times the vector y hat minus y bar times 1. 1 is the vector of ones. In the picture, y minus y bar 1 is the black arrow, and y hat minus y bar 1 is the green arrow in the plane.

22
00:04:47,658 --> 00:05:00,586
y hat is X beta hat. So the cross term is e transpose X beta hat, minus y bar times e transpose 1. The orange arrow is e, from y hat up to y.

23
00:05:01,036 --> 00:05:12,513
From the normal equations, e transpose X is 0. Also, 1 is the first column of X, so e transpose 1 is 0. So the cross term is 0.

24
00:05:12,963 --> 00:05:29,070
So e is perpendicular to the plane. The three arrows make a right triangle. By Pythagoras, the squared length of the black side equals the sum of the other two. That is S S Total equals S S Reg plus S S Res.

25
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:44,240
Slide 19 writes the sums of squares with matrices. J is an n by n matrix of ones. Here is a small example of our own, with n equal to 4 and y equal to 2, 4, 3, 7.

26
00:05:44,690 --> 00:05:52,839
J times y: each row of J adds all the y values. So every entry of J y is 16, the sum.

27
00:05:53,289 --> 00:06:05,833
Divide by n. One over n, J y, has y bar in every entry: 16 over 4 is 4. So one over n J y is y bar times the ones vector.

28
00:06:06,283 --> 00:06:28,747
So y minus one over n J y is the vector of deviations, y i minus y bar. S S Total is its squared length. Expand it. One transpose y is n y bar, so the middle term is minus 2 n y bar squared. The result is y transpose y, minus n y bar squared.

29
00:06:29,197 --> 00:06:51,448
Check with the example. y transpose y is 4 plus 16 plus 9 plus 49, which is 78. n y bar squared is 4 times 16, which is 64. 78 minus 64 is 14. The deviations are minus 2, 0, minus 1 and 3. Their squares add to 14 too.

30
00:06:51,898 --> 00:07:10,330
S S Reg is the sum of y hat i minus y bar, squared. y hat is H y, and y bar 1 is one over n J y. So S S Reg is the squared length of H y minus one over n J y: the green side of the triangle.

31
00:07:10,780 --> 00:07:17,265
Expand the product, in the same way as before. The middle term has 1 transpose H y.

32
00:07:17,715 --> 00:07:38,686
From Lecture 6, H is symmetric and idempotent, so H transpose H is H. Also, 1 is a column of X, so H times 1 is 1: the projection of a vector that is already in the plane is the same vector. So 1 transpose H y is 1 transpose y, which is n y bar.

33
00:07:39,136 --> 00:07:44,597
So S S Reg equals y transpose H y, minus n y bar squared.

34
00:07:45,047 --> 00:08:05,143
S S Res is the squared length of y minus H y: the orange side. It equals y transpose, I minus H, y. Lecture 7 used this form. Check: S S Reg plus S S Res is y transpose y minus n y bar squared, which is S S Total.

35
00:08:05,593 --> 00:08:22,873
Slide 24 computes the three sums for the mercury model M1, with fishpart and weight. S S Total, the total variation of MeHg around its mean, is 1091.77. The purple bar shows it.

36
00:08:23,323 --> 00:08:36,187
The model splits this bar into two parts. S S Reg, in green, is 263.74. S S Res, in orange, is 828.03.

37
00:08:36,637 --> 00:08:51,720
Check: 263.74 plus 828.03 is 1091.77. The R output on slide 24 shows the same total two times.

38
00:08:52,170 --> 00:09:07,637
Slide 20 defines the coefficient of determination, R squared. It is S S Reg over S S Total. Because the two parts add to the total, it is also 1 minus S S Res over S S Total.

39
00:09:08,087 --> 00:09:31,084
For the mercury model, R squared is 263.74 over 1091.77, which is 0.2416. In the bar, it is the green part as a fraction of the whole bar. The R output on slide 24 shows Multiple R-squared, 0.2416.

40
00:09:31,534 --> 00:09:44,505
So R squared is the proportion of the variability in y that the regression model explains. Fishpart and weight explain about 24 percent of the variation in MeHg.

41
00:09:44,955 --> 00:10:06,544
Slide 20 asks how to interpret R squared equal to 1, and R squared equal to 0. Pause the video and try it. R squared is 1 when S S Res is 0. Then every residual is 0, and every point is on the line y equals y hat. The model explains all the variability.

42
00:10:06,994 --> 00:10:24,722
R squared is 0 when S S Reg is 0. Then every fitted value equals y bar: all points are above the same fitted value. The covariates explain none of the variability. Most real models, like the mercury model, are in between.

43
00:10:25,172 --> 00:10:32,873
Slide 21 tests the whole model. The design matrix has the column of ones and p covariate columns.

44
00:10:33,323 --> 00:10:52,971
The null hypothesis is beta 1 equals beta 2 and so on, up to beta p, all equal to 0. This says: y has no association with any of the covariates. Under this null, the model is beta 0 plus epsilon, and every fitted value is y bar.

45
00:10:53,421 --> 00:11:10,040
The test uses the decomposition. F is S S Reg over p, divided by S S Res over n minus p plus 1. Under the null, F has an F distribution with p and n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

46
00:11:10,490 --> 00:11:28,047
If F is large, the model explains a large part of the variation. That is evidence of an association with some of the covariates. We reject the null hypothesis when the p-value is less than alpha. A large F does not say which covariates matter.

47
00:11:28,497 --> 00:11:55,207
Slide 22 shows why. Recall: if X 1 is chi squared with nu 1 degrees of freedom, X 2 is chi squared with nu 2, and they are independent, then X 1 over nu 1, divided by X 2 over nu 2, has an F distribution. So we need three facts. Fact 1: under the null, S S Reg over sigma squared is chi squared with p degrees of freedom. The next slide sketches it.

48
00:11:55,657 --> 00:12:08,009
Fact 2: S S Res over sigma squared is e transpose e over sigma squared. Lecture 7 showed that it is chi squared with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

49
00:12:08,459 --> 00:12:21,408
Fact 3: S S Reg and S S Res are independent. S S Reg uses y hat, which is X beta hat. S S Res uses e. And beta hat and e are independent.

50
00:12:21,858 --> 00:12:35,789
Put the two chi squared variables into the ratio. sigma squared is in the top and in the bottom, so it cancels. The result is the F statistic, with p and n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

51
00:12:36,239 --> 00:13:06,212
Slide 23 sketches fact 1. Write S S Reg as y transpose, H minus one over n J, y. The plan is the same as the proof in Lecture 7. Eigendecompose the matrix. It is idempotent, so its eigenvalues are 0 or 1. To count the ones, use the trace: the sum of the eigenvalues equals the trace. The trace of H minus one over n J is the trace of H minus the trace of one over n J.

52
00:13:06,662 --> 00:13:20,763
The diagonal of one over n J has n entries, each one over n. So its trace is 1. The trace of H is the trace of X, times X transpose X inverse X transpose.

53
00:13:21,213 --> 00:13:40,285
The trace does not change when we move a factor from the front to the back. X times the rest is n by n. The rest times X is p plus 1 by p plus 1, and it is the identity. Its trace is p plus 1. So the trace is p plus 1 minus 1, which is p.

54
00:13:40,735 --> 00:13:56,501
So exactly p eigenvalues are 1. As in Lecture 7, S S Reg over sigma squared is then a sum of p squared independent standard normal variables under the null. It is chi squared with p degrees of freedom.

55
00:13:56,951 --> 00:14:20,759
Slide 24 tests the whole mercury model M1. The null hypothesis says all four covariate coefficients are 0: the three fishpart indicators and weight. n is 135, and p is 4, so n minus p plus 1 is 130. The null distribution is F with 4 and 130 degrees of freedom.

56
00:14:21,209 --> 00:14:40,750
S S Reg over p is 263.74 over 4, which is 65.94. S S Res over 130 is 828.03 over 130, which is 6.3695.

57
00:14:41,200 --> 00:14:59,461
F is 65.94 over 6.3695, which is 10.35. The red region starts at 2.441, the 95 percent quantile. The blue line is the observed F, far into the red region.

58
00:14:59,911 --> 00:15:14,717
The p-value is the area beyond 10.35. It is 2.6 times 10 to the minus 7. We reject the null hypothesis. MeHg is associated with some of the covariates.

59
00:15:15,167 --> 00:15:22,740
Slide 24 ends with three lines of the R summary output. Now we can explain almost every number.

60
00:15:23,190 --> 00:15:41,729
Residual standard error is sigma hat. It is the square root of S S Res over n minus p plus 1. That is the square root of 6.3695, which is 2.524. The 130 is the residual degrees of freedom.

61
00:15:42,179 --> 00:15:52,163
Multiple R-squared is S S Reg over S S Total, 0.2416. Adjusted R squared comes in a later lecture.

62
00:15:52,613 --> 00:16:08,677
The F-statistic line is the test of the entire model: F is 10.35, with p equal to 4 and 130 degrees of freedom, and the p-value is 2.613 times 10 to the minus 7.

63
00:16:09,127 --> 00:16:31,911
Slide 25 collects all of this in an ANOVA table. Bold numbers are the mercury model. The regression row has S S Reg, 263.74, with p, which is 4, degrees of freedom. MS means mean square: S S divided by its degrees of freedom. So M S Reg is 65.94.

64
00:16:32,361 --> 00:16:49,150
The residual row has S S Res, 828.03, with n minus p plus 1, which is 130, degrees of freedom. Its mean square is 6.3695. This is sigma hat squared.

65
00:16:49,600 --> 00:17:00,864
F is the regression mean square over the residual mean square: 65.94 over 6.3695, which is 10.35.

66
00:17:01,314 --> 00:17:27,959
The total row has S S Total, 1091.77, with n minus 1, which is 134, degrees of freedom. Both the S S column and the degrees of freedom column add up: 4 plus 130 is 134. The total has n minus 1 degrees of freedom, because the model has p covariates plus a column of ones for the intercept.

67
00:17:28,409 --> 00:17:36,921
Slide 26 tests a group of covariates. Take the full model with three covariates, x 1, x 2 and x 3.

68
00:17:37,371 --> 00:17:52,006
Split the columns of X into two blocks. X dagger has the columns we keep, here the ones and x 1. X star has the q columns we test, here x 2 and x 3. beta splits in the same way.

69
00:17:52,456 --> 00:18:06,600
The null hypothesis is beta star equals 0. Here, beta 2 equals beta 3 equals 0, so q is 2. This is the same as leaving the columns of X star out of the model.

70
00:18:07,050 --> 00:18:21,599
So fit two models. The full model has all the columns. The reduced model has only X dagger. It is the model under the null hypothesis. Here it is beta 0 plus beta 1 x i 1.

71
00:18:22,049 --> 00:18:27,767
The question is: does the full model explain much more variability than the reduced model?

72
00:18:28,217 --> 00:18:49,571
Slide 27 compares the two fits. The bars use the mercury data: the reduced model has weight only. Both bars have the same length, S S Total, because y is the same. The reduced model has S S Reg 187.17 and S S Res 904.61.

73
00:18:50,021 --> 00:19:03,717
The full model adds the three fishpart indicators. Its S S Reg is larger, 263.74, and its S S Res is smaller, 828.03.

74
00:19:04,167 --> 00:19:28,445
The red box is the extra variation that the q added covariates explain: S S Reg of the full model, minus S S Reg of the reduced model. Here it is 76.58. By the same logic as before, under the null, this over sigma squared is chi squared with q degrees of freedom. q is the difference in the number of parameters.

75
00:19:28,895 --> 00:19:41,993
Divide by q, and divide by the residual mean square of the full model. Under the null, this F has an F distribution with q and n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

76
00:19:42,443 --> 00:20:02,027
Slide 28 says: we saw this test before. In Lecture 9, the F statistic was beta star hat transpose, times V star inverse, times beta star hat, over q, divided by sigma hat squared. It measures how far the estimates are from the null value 0.

77
00:20:02,477 --> 00:20:09,859
The new version uses sums of squares. It measures how much extra variability the q covariates explain.

78
00:20:10,309 --> 00:20:27,802
The two F statistics are exactly equal. On slide 24, the R code computes both for the mercury data, and both give 4.007444. Assignment 3 asks why they are equal, so this video does not show it.

79
00:20:28,252 --> 00:20:50,951
Slides 24 and 25 ask: is fishpart associated with MeHg, in the presence of weight? The full model has fishpart and weight, with 130 residual degrees of freedom. The reduced model has weight only, with 133. Under the null, all three fishpart coefficients are 0.

80
00:20:51,401 --> 00:20:59,614
q is 133 minus 130, which is 3. This is the difference in the number of parameters.

81
00:21:00,064 --> 00:21:21,483
The extra regression sum of squares is 263.74 minus 187.17. Divide by 3 to get 25.525. Divide by the residual mean square of the full model, 6.3695. F is 4.007.

82
00:21:21,933 --> 00:21:50,263
The 95 percent quantile of F with 3 and 130 degrees of freedom is 2.674. The observed F is in the red region. The p-value is 0.009128, less than 0.05. We reject the null hypothesis. Fishpart is associated with MeHg, given weight. Video 19 found the same p-value with the Lecture 9 formula.

83
00:21:50,713 --> 00:21:57,433
Slide 25 also shows a table that R prints for two models. Each row is one model.

84
00:21:57,883 --> 00:22:17,766
Res dot Df is the residual degrees of freedom: 133 and 130. RSS means residual sum of squares, which is S S Res: 904.61 for the reduced model and 828.03 for the full model.

85
00:22:18,216 --> 00:22:48,915
Sum of Sq is the drop in RSS: 904.61 minus 828.03 is 76.576. This is the same as the extra regression sum of squares, because both models have the same S S Total. In the bars, the red box is the same piece. Df is 3, F is 4.0074, and the p-value is 0.009128, as before.

86
00:22:49,365 --> 00:23:11,039
Slide 31 uses the same method for a larger class of null hypotheses: general linear hypotheses. Each has the form C beta equals 0, where C is a matrix of rank r. Example: in a model with four covariates, test beta 1 equals beta 2 equals 0.

87
00:23:11,489 --> 00:23:19,511
Write one row of C for each restriction. C has 5 columns, one for each of beta 0 to beta 4.

88
00:23:19,961 --> 00:23:29,390
Row 1 is 0, 1, 0, 0, 0. Times beta, it gives beta 1. So row 1 says beta 1 equals 0.

89
00:23:29,840 --> 00:23:40,869
Row 2 is 0, 0, 1, 0, 0. It gives beta 2 equals 0. The two rows are not multiples of each other, so the rank r is 2.

90
00:23:41,319 --> 00:24:01,138
Slide 33 gives the test. Fit the full model, and the reduced model under C beta equals 0. The F statistic is the same as before, with r in place of q. Under the null, it has an F distribution with r and n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

91
00:24:01,588 --> 00:24:14,345
Slide 34 lists the tests we now have. t tests are for one number: one coefficient, beta j equals theta 0, or one linear combination of coefficients.

92
00:24:14,795 --> 00:24:31,094
F tests are for several restrictions at the same time: a joint null, beta star equals 0, or a general linear hypothesis, C beta equals 0. Each F test compares a full model and a reduced model.

93
00:24:31,544 --> 00:24:40,248
The test of the entire model is one case: X star has all p covariates, and the reduced model has only the intercept.

94
00:24:40,698 --> 00:24:56,805
Practice question 1. An ANOVA table has some missing values. Part a: how many observations? Part b: what is S S Total? Part c: test that all coefficients except the intercept are 0. Pause the video and try it.

95
00:24:57,255 --> 00:25:14,620
Start with the regression degrees of freedom. MS is S S over its degrees of freedom. So the degrees of freedom are S S over MS: 138.75 over 46.25, which is 3. So p is 3.

96
00:25:15,070 --> 00:25:36,403
The degrees of freedom add up. The total is 3 plus 131, which is 134. The total degrees of freedom are n minus 1, so n is 135. This answers part a. Check: n minus p plus 1 is 135 minus 4, which is 131.

97
00:25:36,853 --> 00:25:50,443
Part b. The sums of squares add up. S S Total is 138.75 plus 953.02, which is 1091.77.

98
00:25:50,893 --> 00:26:02,775
Part c needs F. F is the regression mean square over the residual mean square: 46.25 over 7.275, which is 6.36.

99
00:26:03,225 --> 00:26:17,412
Part c. The null hypothesis is beta 1 equals beta 2 equals beta 3 equals 0. Under this null, F has an F distribution with 3 and 131 degrees of freedom.

100
00:26:17,862 --> 00:26:28,358
The 95 percent quantile of F with 3 and 131 degrees of freedom is 2.674. The red region starts there.

101
00:26:28,808 --> 00:26:48,264
The observed F is 6.36, larger than 2.674. The p-value is 0.000467. We reject the null hypothesis at the 0.05 level. This matches the solution on slide 37.

102
00:26:48,714 --> 00:27:06,207
Practice question 2. The model has four covariates. How would you test beta 1 equals beta 2, and beta 3 equals beta 4? Pause the video and try it. First, write it as a general linear hypothesis, C beta equals 0.

103
00:27:06,657 --> 00:27:19,223
Row 1 of C is 0, 1, minus 1, 0, 0. Times beta, it gives beta 1 minus beta 2. Setting it to 0 gives beta 1 equals beta 2.

104
00:27:19,673 --> 00:27:32,707
Row 2 is 0, 0, 0, 1, minus 1. It gives beta 3 minus beta 4 equals 0. The two rows are not multiples of each other, so the rank r is 2.

105
00:27:33,157 --> 00:27:47,557
So we can use the F test of slide 33, with r equal to 2. Under the null, F has an F distribution with 2 and n minus 5 degrees of freedom. Next: how to fit the reduced model.

106
00:27:48,007 --> 00:28:05,949
Slide 39 fits the reduced model. Under the null, beta 2 equals beta 1 and beta 4 equals beta 3. Put these into the model. The columns x 1 and x 2 now have the same coefficient, and so do x 3 and x 4.

107
00:28:06,399 --> 00:28:24,895
Group the terms. The reduced model is beta 0, plus beta 1 times x i 1 plus x i 2, plus beta 3 times x i 3 plus x i 4. In the picture, the blue columns add to one column, and the orange columns add to another.

108
00:28:25,345 --> 00:28:42,262
So we fit the reduced model with two new covariates: the sums. Then the F statistic uses the extra regression sum of squares over r equal to 2, and the residual mean square of the full model, with n minus 5 degrees of freedom.

109
00:28:42,712 --> 00:29:00,653
Practice question 3 uses the same model. How would you test C beta equals 0 for this C, and what does it test? Pause the video and try it. C has 3 rows. Each row has 1 in the column of beta 1, and minus 1 in one other column.

110
00:29:01,103 --> 00:29:07,631
Row 1 gives beta 1 minus beta 2 equals 0, so beta 1 equals beta 2.

111
00:29:08,081 --> 00:29:11,260
Row 2 gives beta 1 equals beta 3.

112
00:29:11,710 --> 00:29:15,038
Row 3 gives beta 1 equals beta 4.

113
00:29:15,488 --> 00:29:30,101
Together: beta 1 equals beta 2 equals beta 3 equals beta 4. The null says all four slopes are equal. The three rows are not linear combinations of each other, so the rank r is 3.

114
00:29:30,551 --> 00:29:43,501
Slide 41 fits the reduced model. Put beta 2, beta 3 and beta 4 equal to beta 1 into the model. All four covariate columns now have the same coefficient.

115
00:29:43,951 --> 00:29:59,865
Group the terms. The reduced model is beta 0 plus beta 1 times the sum of the four covariates. So it is a simple linear regression on the new covariate x tilde i, the sum of x i 1 to x i 4.

116
00:30:00,315 --> 00:30:10,363
Then use the F test with r equal to 3. Under the null, F has an F distribution with 3 and n minus 5 degrees of freedom.

117
00:30:10,813 --> 00:30:49,463
Here is a tutorial question with the ANOVA view. Tutorial 3, question 1 c. The model fit 1 has salary and the district size as a factor with 3 levels. Test that mean salary does not differ among the three sizes. The output gives Multiple R-squared 0.3909, and an F-statistic of 103.3 on 2 and 322 degrees of freedom. Pause the video and try it. In the bar, R squared is the green part of S S Total, and 1 minus R squared is the orange part.

118
00:30:49,913 --> 00:31:14,105
The null hypothesis is beta 1 equals beta 2 equals 0. These are all the coefficients except the intercept. So this is the test of the entire model, with p equal to 2 and 322 residual degrees of freedom. The posted solution uses the Lecture 9 formula. By slide 28, it gives the same F.

119
00:31:14,555 --> 00:31:50,027
Divide the top and the bottom of F by S S Total. S S Reg over S S Total is R squared, and S S Res over S S Total is 1 minus R squared. So F is 0.3909 over 2, divided by 0.6091 over 322. That is 0.19545 over 0.0018916, which is 103.3. This is the F-statistic line of the output.

120
00:31:50,477 --> 00:32:12,045
103.3 is much larger than 3.024, the 95 percent quantile of F with 2 and 322 degrees of freedom. The p-value is less than 2.2 times 10 to the minus 16. We reject the null hypothesis. The district size matters.

121
00:32:12,495 --> 00:32:27,940
To recap. The total sum of squares splits into the regression part and the residual part. The cross term is 0, because the residuals are perpendicular to the columns of X. Each sum also has a matrix form.

122
00:32:28,390 --> 00:32:48,337
R squared is S S Reg over S S Total: the proportion of the variability that the model explains. The F test of the entire model divides the regression mean square by the residual mean square. In the ANOVA table, the sums of squares and the degrees of freedom add up.

